&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; Okay, Stewart.

00:01:21.965 --> 00:01:23.470
&gt;&gt; Feeling pretty good, it's number one.

00:01:23.470 --> 00:01:27.380
&gt;&gt; It's number one, so
you were not born Stewart Butterfield.

00:01:27.380 --> 00:01:32.230
&gt;&gt; I was born Dharma Jeremy Butterfield
to hippy parents in a little town called

00:01:32.230 --> 00:01:35.000
Lund in British, Columbia which
is literally the end of the road.

00:01:35.000 --> 00:01:39.225
So Pacific Coast Highway is the same road
that goes all the way down to Chile.

00:01:39.225 --> 00:01:42.868
And then all the way up to where
the Fjords of British Columbia,

00:01:42.868 --> 00:01:47.614
and the fact that there's no really,
there's no people within any further north

00:01:47.614 --> 00:01:50.953
means that it was foolish to
continue building the road.

00:01:50.953 --> 00:01:54.770
So, born there and grew up in a log
cabin for the first couple of years.

00:01:54.770 --> 00:01:56.090
&gt;&gt; Grew up in a log cabin?

00:01:56.090 --> 00:01:57.370
Abe Lincoln also grew up in a log cabin.

00:01:57.370 --> 00:01:58.017
&gt;&gt; Very, yeah.

00:01:58.017 --> 00:01:59.586
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; That's why I bring it up, for

00:01:59.586 --> 00:02:00.789
the positive associations.

00:02:00.789 --> 00:02:03.950
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH] Yeah, so
trust me, I understand.

00:02:03.950 --> 00:02:06.829
My parents named me
Trevorski Tylon Garrett.

00:02:06.829 --> 00:02:11.100
And as a four-year-old, I was not writing
Trevorski on every spelling test.

00:02:11.100 --> 00:02:13.474
So I go by Tylin, and
you actually know me by Ty.

00:02:13.474 --> 00:02:14.155
&gt;&gt; Yeah.

00:02:14.155 --> 00:02:17.201
&gt;&gt; So-
&gt;&gt; T-Y is very creative.

00:02:17.201 --> 00:02:19.494
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; So when I was 12,

00:02:19.494 --> 00:02:20.630
I really wanted to be normal.

00:02:20.630 --> 00:02:24.439
And for some reason, I thought Stewart
was a normal name, like Mike, or

00:02:24.439 --> 00:02:25.632
something like that.

00:02:25.632 --> 00:02:28.650
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; And Stewart, it's a pretty bad name.

00:02:28.650 --> 00:02:30.000
You'll notice this after I say it.

00:02:30.000 --> 00:02:34.295
Any time you watch a movie, TV show,
there's a character named Stewart,

00:02:34.295 --> 00:02:39.154
there's like the jerk version and there's
like the sad sack kind of loser version.

00:02:39.154 --> 00:02:41.485
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; Which never a protagonist with

00:02:41.485 --> 00:02:43.933
the exception of the mouse Stuart Little.

00:02:43.933 --> 00:02:44.504
&gt;&gt; Stuart Little.

00:02:44.504 --> 00:02:45.828
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; That was one of my

00:02:45.828 --> 00:02:47.390
favorite movies growing up.

00:02:47.390 --> 00:02:49.700
So if you could pick any name today,
what would it be?

00:02:49.700 --> 00:02:53.022
&gt;&gt; It would be Dharma Jeremy Butterfield,
it's a sick name.

00:02:53.022 --> 00:02:54.961
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; Cool, so

00:02:54.961 --> 00:02:59.010
you hinted a little bit on your parents.

00:03:00.290 --> 00:03:05.996
What have been the most lasting
influences from their parenting?

00:03:05.996 --> 00:03:06.910
&gt;&gt; That's a good question.

00:03:06.910 --> 00:03:11.480
I mean, it's hard to separate out

00:03:11.480 --> 00:03:13.899
that from what life would've
been without those influences.

00:03:15.040 --> 00:03:20.650
My mom is incredibly supportive
to the extent that when I was 16,

00:03:20.650 --> 00:03:25.875
I got in a car accident,
just totaled the car, my dad's car.

00:03:25.875 --> 00:03:29.117
And my mom's reaction was, well,
it's really good that you did that,

00:03:29.117 --> 00:03:32.003
because you learned an important
lesson about driving safety.

00:03:32.003 --> 00:03:36.996
[LAUGH] Which is not
the reaction I was expecting.

00:03:36.996 --> 00:03:42.782
My dad was a real estate developer, and
real estate development usually works,

00:03:42.782 --> 00:03:48.240
the people incorporating new entity for
each project or each development.

00:03:48.240 --> 00:03:51.758
Maybe there's a management company
that takes measuring fees but

00:03:51.758 --> 00:03:53.767
it kind of isolates the investments,

00:03:53.767 --> 00:03:57.619
which means that it's like creating
your business over and over again.

00:03:57.619 --> 00:04:00.959
Like every two or three years,
there's a fundraising cycle, and

00:04:00.959 --> 00:04:03.778
there's kind of putting
together the vision and a plan.

00:04:03.778 --> 00:04:07.060
And then over the next decade or
so, that plays out.

00:04:07.060 --> 00:04:09.898
So I think that was a big influence for
me,

00:04:09.898 --> 00:04:14.371
just because I got to see
the development of, I don't even know.

00:04:14.371 --> 00:04:18.130
Over the course of my childhood,
once I was aware of what was going on,

00:04:18.130 --> 00:04:20.377
maybe five or six different businesses.

00:04:20.377 --> 00:04:22.109
And that was good practice,

00:04:22.109 --> 00:04:25.640
because I think I started
looking at the world that way.

00:04:25.640 --> 00:04:27.690
&gt;&gt; That's cool, that's cool.

00:04:27.690 --> 00:04:29.770
So you were born Dharma Butterfield.

00:04:30.810 --> 00:04:33.880
You were raised by parents who
aspired to live off the land.

00:04:33.880 --> 00:04:38.400
It only makes sense that you
would be drawn to technology.

00:04:38.400 --> 00:04:42.050
So what led you to teach yourself
to program at the age of seven?

00:04:45.310 --> 00:04:49.451
&gt;&gt; Computers were just so cool and
even like you see now, any three-month or

00:04:49.451 --> 00:04:52.600
six-month old are just drawn
to the iPad in a way that

00:04:54.420 --> 00:04:57.660
seems like it must be indicative of
a lower level brain function that was

00:04:57.660 --> 00:05:00.650
hijacked in order to be attracted to this.

00:05:00.650 --> 00:05:03.690
So for me, many screen,

00:05:03.690 --> 00:05:06.570
just like any child,
any screen was just super attractive.

00:05:06.570 --> 00:05:10.475
But the idea that you could control
what appeared was really magical.

00:05:10.475 --> 00:05:17.720
And it was 1979, 1980, somewhere around
there, I got an Apple TV at home.

00:05:17.720 --> 00:05:20.050
So we had one in the classroom.

00:05:20.050 --> 00:05:25.090
So the very first class at my school
to have a computer in the classroom.

00:05:25.090 --> 00:05:29.310
And I would buy a copy of a magazine
called Bite which in the back, a couple of

00:05:29.310 --> 00:05:33.240
pages had programs that you can just
type out yourself in an Apple basic.

00:05:33.240 --> 00:05:38.610
And you can change a couple things, and
see what happened, and it was really,

00:05:38.610 --> 00:05:44.170
I have difficulty describing like
why it had such a powerful hold.

00:05:44.170 --> 00:05:49.634
But what was interesting is if
you fast forward like 20 years,

00:05:49.634 --> 00:05:55.120
there was, I had an early getting
console called television.

00:05:55.120 --> 00:05:59.410
So like video games, like most boys my
age, there are arcades where you went and

00:05:59.410 --> 00:06:01.610
put quarters in machines and
stuff like that.

00:06:01.610 --> 00:06:03.490
But computers themselves became less and

00:06:03.490 --> 00:06:06.340
less interesting to me over the course of
high school but when I got to college,

00:06:06.340 --> 00:06:09.910
I got an account on the school's Unix
machine and discovered the Internet.

00:06:09.910 --> 00:06:15.422
This was 1992, and
that was just totally mind expanding.

00:06:15.422 --> 00:06:19.062
And I almost couldn't believe that
such a thing was possible, and

00:06:19.062 --> 00:06:22.860
had like the same feeling of wonder,
but to a higher degree.

00:06:22.860 --> 00:06:25.960
Because it was like we as a species,

00:06:25.960 --> 00:06:30.650
had developed the ability to
transcend geography in a much more

00:06:30.650 --> 00:06:34.140
profound way than like long distance
phone calls had or the telegraph had.

00:06:34.140 --> 00:06:37.750
You could find community,
I grew up in between College in Victoria,

00:06:37.750 --> 00:06:40.720
British Columbia, which is again on
the edge of the continent, very remote,

00:06:40.720 --> 00:06:41.580
kind of provincial.

00:06:43.550 --> 00:06:46.806
And you could find people who were
interested in exactly what you were,

00:06:46.806 --> 00:06:48.053
anywhere in the world, and

00:06:48.053 --> 00:06:50.673
that communication was happening
at the speed of light.

00:06:50.673 --> 00:06:55.988
So that really opened it up, and
then fast forward another ten years.

00:06:55.988 --> 00:06:57.938
So like in the early 2000s,

00:06:57.938 --> 00:07:01.767
I had this experience finally
where I had my laptop with me, and

00:07:01.767 --> 00:07:06.174
at one point in my life had been like
the Steve Jobs bicycle for the mind but

00:07:06.174 --> 00:07:11.478
this incredible machine that anything was
possible, and all this amazing software.

00:07:11.478 --> 00:07:15.929
When it wasn't connected to the Internet
was inert, was basically kind of useless,

00:07:15.929 --> 00:07:17.400
it was like a rock.

00:07:17.400 --> 00:07:19.470
So it's a very interesting
experience to think about,

00:07:19.470 --> 00:07:24.260
like the successive layers
of what really matters.

00:07:24.260 --> 00:07:28.967
The first one being that ability to
run arbitrary code to generate more or

00:07:28.967 --> 00:07:31.601
less anything that a human can imagine.

00:07:31.601 --> 00:07:33.680
And then the ability to
put all of those together.

00:07:33.680 --> 00:07:38.040
And I think that was like the thing
that has guided my career ever since,

00:07:38.040 --> 00:07:42.080
is the exploration of that idea of
computing technology as a means of

00:07:42.080 --> 00:07:44.460
facilitating human interaction.

00:07:44.460 --> 00:07:45.220
&gt;&gt; It's amazing.

00:07:45.220 --> 00:07:49.080
So, that's what drew you to
technology initially, but

00:07:49.080 --> 00:07:51.330
you actually studied philosophy.

00:07:51.330 --> 00:07:53.430
So what inspired that decision?

00:07:54.760 --> 00:07:57.770
&gt;&gt; I really wanted to do
a degree in cognitive science,

00:07:57.770 --> 00:08:00.720
but the school that I went to
didn't have cognitive science.

00:08:00.720 --> 00:08:05.251
So, cognitive science is usually computer
science, psychology, linguistics,

00:08:05.251 --> 00:08:06.770
and psychology.

00:08:06.770 --> 00:08:10.280
And so
I wanted to take courses in all four.

00:08:11.350 --> 00:08:14.520
To do an honors degree in Psychology
was like every single course was

00:08:14.520 --> 00:08:15.280
a requirement.

00:08:15.280 --> 00:08:17.260
In fact, you had to do extra.

00:08:17.260 --> 00:08:20.590
Where as philosophy,
it's pretty light set of requirements,

00:08:20.590 --> 00:08:22.520
even to do an honors degree,
so I chose philosophy.

00:08:22.520 --> 00:08:24.900
Literally like that,
it was like of those four,

00:08:24.900 --> 00:08:27.002
the one that had the fewest requirements.

00:08:27.002 --> 00:08:32.291
But after I started studying it,
even though I suck is really a philosophy

00:08:32.291 --> 00:08:36.670
of mine is really interested in
neuro psych as an undergrad.

00:08:36.670 --> 00:08:40.510
The fundamentals of philosophy
I found super fascinating and

00:08:40.510 --> 00:08:46.170
this sounds bad and it is bad in one
sense and it's good in another sense.

00:08:46.170 --> 00:08:50.000
So you think about the last
2,500 years of kind of

00:08:50.000 --> 00:08:52.830
the history of inquiry
of all different kinds.

00:08:52.830 --> 00:08:54.430
At some point, everyone was a philosopher.

00:08:55.570 --> 00:08:58.500
If you were interested in the world in

00:08:58.500 --> 00:09:02.940
a religious way like the beginnings
of science, philosophy.

00:09:02.940 --> 00:09:06.090
At some point, mathematics, geometry,

00:09:06.090 --> 00:09:10.121
astronomy split off over
the next many hundred years.

00:09:10.121 --> 00:09:14.345
Things like biology in the 19th century
split off into its own discipline and

00:09:14.345 --> 00:09:18.633
psychology, anthropology, sociology,
computer science, linguistics,

00:09:18.633 --> 00:09:20.170
women's studies.

00:09:20.170 --> 00:09:24.805
Until all you had left was an area
of inquiry that is not directed

00:09:24.805 --> 00:09:28.491
at anything except for
like itself and language.

00:09:28.491 --> 00:09:31.130
So in one sense, it's really boring.

00:09:31.130 --> 00:09:35.426
So, if you didn't ever study philosophy,
and you pick up a book of contemporary

00:09:35.426 --> 00:09:41.560
Anglo American analytic philosophy,
it is super, super boring.

00:09:41.560 --> 00:09:45.350
It's almost impenetrable
without this giant vocabulary

00:09:45.350 --> 00:09:47.130
of ways you're going to get into it.

00:09:47.130 --> 00:09:52.117
But once you're into it,
I still find it really fascinating,

00:09:52.117 --> 00:09:56.459
because there's so
many unanswerable questions, yeah.

00:09:56.459 --> 00:10:00.279
&gt;&gt; That's good to know, so who was
the most influential philosopher to you?

00:10:07.066 --> 00:10:11.050
&gt;&gt; I have a pretty broad range and
I liked all kinds of thinkers but

00:10:11.050 --> 00:10:17.210
like go back to Aristotle, Spinoza, more
contemporary, Klein and Donald Davidson.

00:10:17.210 --> 00:10:22.601
But if there's one, it was Decker because
that's why I ended up going to Cambridge.

00:10:22.601 --> 00:10:24.136
&gt;&gt; Amazing, amazing.

00:10:24.136 --> 00:10:27.140
So we're in 1997 now.

00:10:27.140 --> 00:10:31.790
You're armed with two philosophy degrees
and a name for philosopher, Butterfield.

00:10:32.790 --> 00:10:34.420
But you decided to become a web developer.

00:10:35.940 --> 00:10:37.850
What led you back into technology?

00:10:37.850 --> 00:10:44.300
&gt;&gt; Well, so like I said, I got to college
in 92, which, is like, at least for

00:10:44.300 --> 00:10:48.770
my awareness, six months to maybe
a year before the web really took off.

00:10:48.770 --> 00:10:52.030
I think Mosaic had been invented,
but wasn't really widely deployed.

00:10:52.030 --> 00:10:56.320
So the Internet was email, IRC,
a Unix program called Talk.

00:10:56.320 --> 00:10:57.990
More than anything else, it was Usenet.

00:10:59.920 --> 00:11:04.136
And that meant that as soon as
the web became a popular medium,

00:11:04.136 --> 00:11:08.852
that started supplanting things
like Gopher and Ways, I was there.

00:11:08.852 --> 00:11:13.389
And it was really, the HTML back
then was just dead simple, so

00:11:13.389 --> 00:11:15.670
very easy to teach ourselves.

00:11:15.670 --> 00:11:22.173
So 93, I was, I don't know, one of five
people in my hometown who knew HTML.

00:11:22.173 --> 00:11:26.363
Which meant that 94, 95, 96,
every year, my summer job, but

00:11:26.363 --> 00:11:30.059
also just my job during the school
year was making websites for

00:11:30.059 --> 00:11:34.345
people who didn't know how to make
websites because pretty much no one did.

00:11:34.345 --> 00:11:40.126
And it was like 98, I finished my
masters and was enrolled in PhD.

00:11:40.126 --> 00:11:43.711
And it was the beginning.

00:11:43.711 --> 00:11:45.870
So a friend of mine had just
finished his PhD in philosophy.

00:11:47.020 --> 00:11:51.030
And went to a great school and
did great thesis work and

00:11:51.030 --> 00:11:55.260
was really at the top of the range and

00:11:55.260 --> 00:11:58.000
got his first job which was at
the University of Louisville in Kentucky.

00:11:58.000 --> 00:12:00.720
And he really did not want to live
in Kentucky and it was a crappy

00:12:00.720 --> 00:12:05.470
job with low pay and it is sessional
position so it renews every nine months.

00:12:05.470 --> 00:12:08.370
I thought of how many hoops I had to
jump through just to get to that point.

00:12:09.740 --> 00:12:14.640
Or because it was 98, and the .com thing
started to take off, and I knew the web,

00:12:14.640 --> 00:12:19.120
and all of my friends who are early web
people were moving to San Francisco and

00:12:19.120 --> 00:12:22.064
getting jobs that paid two or
three times as much.

00:12:22.064 --> 00:12:24.238
And it was exciting and dynamic and
were changing the world.

00:12:24.238 --> 00:12:28.076
So I had an advice from a couple of
professors over the course of my

00:12:28.076 --> 00:12:32.697
career who were essentially were this
is a terrible life please don't become

00:12:32.697 --> 00:12:33.561
an academic.

00:12:33.561 --> 00:12:35.531
If you're interested in this stuff,

00:12:35.531 --> 00:12:38.799
you can subscribe to the journals and
attend the conferences.

00:12:38.799 --> 00:12:41.962
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; You don't have to actually do a PhD and

00:12:41.962 --> 00:12:43.597
then go be a professor.

00:12:43.597 --> 00:12:44.665
I took that advice.

00:12:44.665 --> 00:12:46.800
&gt;&gt; Okay, [LAUGH] that's good to know.

00:12:46.800 --> 00:12:53.070
So I'm actually curious, raise your
hand if you have a humanities degree.

00:12:54.830 --> 00:12:59.360
Okay, keep your hand raised if you're
considering a career in technology.

00:13:01.480 --> 00:13:06.180
Okay, so my question there is,
what were the advantages and

00:13:06.180 --> 00:13:11.120
disadvantages in having a humanities
degree within the technology industry?

00:13:13.640 --> 00:13:16.410
&gt;&gt; It's tough because there's
multiple technology industries.

00:13:16.410 --> 00:13:20.530
So I would say, when I started in 98,
the web was tech, but

00:13:20.530 --> 00:13:23.930
it was populated much more by people with
a background in like graphic design or

00:13:23.930 --> 00:13:26.680
architecture if you're
making web development.

00:13:26.680 --> 00:13:33.400
This serious back end programmers had a
parallel track to web server development.

00:13:33.400 --> 00:13:38.170
But it was really like a totally
different era and there

00:13:38.170 --> 00:13:42.970
wasn't anything in between the architects
and the graphic designers on one side.

00:13:42.970 --> 00:13:45.860
And the people, this won't be a familiar
reference to many of you, but

00:13:45.860 --> 00:13:50.703
the people are using web logic and
ATG, Dynamo, and

00:13:50.703 --> 00:13:55.330
these like from today's
contemporary perspective,

00:13:55.330 --> 00:13:58.920
kind of really horrible
application servers,

00:13:58.920 --> 00:14:01.430
that had a fundamentally different
approach to doing web development.

00:14:01.430 --> 00:14:04.870
It was stateful applications,
things like Enterprise JavaBeans.

00:14:04.870 --> 00:14:09.630
And so I don't think it really made any
difference, what your background was,

00:14:09.630 --> 00:14:10.210
at that point.

00:14:10.210 --> 00:14:15.680
It could have been history could have
been finance could have been physics.

00:14:17.380 --> 00:14:20.240
And meanwhile, there's a different
technology injury which

00:14:20.240 --> 00:14:24.630
is like all of the descendants
of Fairchild Semiconductor and

00:14:24.630 --> 00:14:29.145
Intel HP and a bunch of companies
that were native to this area.

00:14:29.145 --> 00:14:31.260
But that was completely different.

00:14:31.260 --> 00:14:35.585
Like, the design of circuit boards and
processors and

00:14:35.585 --> 00:14:38.345
manufacturing computers was
totally unrelated to the web.

00:14:38.345 --> 00:14:41.815
And still today,
I think we say tech industry broadly.

00:14:41.815 --> 00:14:46.790
I think mostly what people mean
is at least around the Bay Area

00:14:46.790 --> 00:14:49.860
is companies that receive EC backing
as opposed to anything else.

00:14:49.860 --> 00:14:53.050
They're not necessarily
specifically technology companies.

00:14:53.050 --> 00:14:57.490
And the side effect of
software eating the world,

00:14:57.490 --> 00:15:02.140
in the famous phrase, is that every
company's a technology company.

00:15:02.140 --> 00:15:07.070
So you can look at, I don't know,
Visa or Mastercard probably

00:15:07.070 --> 00:15:12.150
employ close to an order of magnitude
more software developers than Stripe.

00:15:13.368 --> 00:15:16.300
Everyone would say Stripe
as a technology company,

00:15:17.440 --> 00:15:20.220
is PayPal a financial services company or
a tech company?

00:15:20.220 --> 00:15:23.410
Is Airbnb a tech company or
a hospitality company?

00:15:23.410 --> 00:15:27.930
It really becomes increasingly
hard to make that distinction,

00:15:27.930 --> 00:15:32.785
unless you mean like technology is
Huawei making 5G antenna chips,

00:15:32.785 --> 00:15:37.347
and it's Dell, and
then software businesses, like.

00:15:37.347 --> 00:15:39.200
&gt;&gt; Yeah, thank you for that.

00:15:39.200 --> 00:15:43.920
So you worked as a web designer for a few
years before starting a video game company

00:15:43.920 --> 00:15:47.130
and launching your first video game,
Game Neverending.

00:15:47.130 --> 00:15:49.240
What was your vision for that game?

00:15:49.240 --> 00:15:52.330
&gt;&gt; So, when you said 92 the thing that

00:15:52.330 --> 00:15:54.700
was most interesting to me
about the Internet was Usenet.

00:15:54.700 --> 00:15:57.800
Usenet is a,
those of you who don't know it,

00:15:57.800 --> 00:16:01.850
a hierarchical directory of newsgroups and
it covered more or less everything.

00:16:01.850 --> 00:16:06.696
So, they began with the three letter
Abbreviations, so SCI was science,

00:16:06.696 --> 00:16:10.169
and there was science, physics,
and geology, and so on.

00:16:10.169 --> 00:16:14.839
And then Rak, rack.music.gdad,
Grateful Dead was,

00:16:14.839 --> 00:16:19.797
in 92, the Netflix of its time
in the sense that it used more

00:16:19.797 --> 00:16:24.720
bandwidth than any other
single thing on the Internet.

00:16:24.720 --> 00:16:29.760
There's so much traffic of
people discussing Grateful Dead,

00:16:29.760 --> 00:16:32.330
that it kind of surpass everything else.

00:16:32.330 --> 00:16:37.710
So 93, I guess,
probably a year later, I had

00:16:37.710 --> 00:16:40.990
the first experience of having a crush on
someone that I had never actually met, it

00:16:40.990 --> 00:16:46.390
was just from her online persona, her sig
files, the things she said in comments.

00:16:46.390 --> 00:16:51.630
And that the idea that that kind
of connection was possible,

00:16:51.630 --> 00:16:57.050
I mean at this really early stage but
it's very interesting to me.

00:16:57.050 --> 00:17:00.810
You go forward to 98, 99,
2000, and people had blogs,

00:17:00.810 --> 00:17:04.670
they were the early social networks
like Six Degrees, and then

00:17:06.020 --> 00:17:10.880
Friendster was probably 2002-ish,
around there.

00:17:10.880 --> 00:17:15.400
But people had started kind of developing
a persona and having interactions with

00:17:15.400 --> 00:17:20.070
other people over the Internet in a bunch
of different virtual communities.

00:17:20.070 --> 00:17:21.440
Some of those are really explicit,

00:17:21.440 --> 00:17:26.740
like the wells are, like more the ancient
ones but bulletin board systems,

00:17:26.740 --> 00:17:32.835
discussion boards moves kind of
like interactive chat based games.

00:17:32.835 --> 00:17:36.720
And I mean I said earlier that

00:17:36.720 --> 00:17:40.540
the idea of social interaction mediated
by computing technology like the new

00:17:40.540 --> 00:17:43.900
possibilities that opens up was the thing
that is really fascinating to me.

00:17:43.900 --> 00:17:48.980
So when you say game, I think people have
the assumption that there's puzzle games,

00:17:48.980 --> 00:17:52.670
and there's shooting games, and
there's sports games, and stuff like that.

00:17:52.670 --> 00:17:56.110
This was none of those and this was just
play as a pretext for social attraction,

00:17:56.110 --> 00:18:00.560
so this is a description that may or
may not have appeal to some of you.

00:18:00.560 --> 00:18:03.365
I will tell you that it does not
have broad commercial appeal.

00:18:03.365 --> 00:18:08.160
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; And that's just like a whimsical

00:18:08.160 --> 00:18:13.057
world of absurdist humor and
kind of hopefully,

00:18:13.057 --> 00:18:17.138
delightful little things to discover.

00:18:17.138 --> 00:18:19.940
But it's mostly like a venue for
people to interact into and

00:18:19.940 --> 00:18:22.780
to form community with one another.

00:18:22.780 --> 00:18:27.510
And super popular among the small
group of people from we developed this

00:18:27.510 --> 00:18:31.010
prototype and we're testing it,
but that was 2002.

00:18:31.010 --> 00:18:35.153
So some of you are probably were
just little babies in 2002, but

00:18:35.153 --> 00:18:38.641
there was the dot-com crash
which started in 2000,

00:18:38.641 --> 00:18:43.530
there was the WorldCom, and
Enron accounting scandals, there was 9/11.

00:18:43.530 --> 00:18:48.707
It was just like a really dark time for
financial markets, the Nasdaq was down,

00:18:48.707 --> 00:18:53.739
I think, 80 or 85%, from its peak,
so the S&amp;P 500 was down 65%.

00:18:53.739 --> 00:18:57.319
It's kind of hard to imagine,
even in comparison to 2008,

00:18:57.319 --> 00:19:00.491
so no one wanted to invest in
Internet stuff period, but

00:19:00.491 --> 00:19:04.948
definitely no one wanted to invest in
web based massively multiplayer games,

00:19:04.948 --> 00:19:07.940
that was just as frivolous
as you could possibly be.

00:19:07.940 --> 00:19:10.180
Which meant that no one
would invest in us and

00:19:10.180 --> 00:19:11.410
we didn't have enough money to finish it.

00:19:11.410 --> 00:19:12.630
And we tried to cast around for

00:19:12.630 --> 00:19:16.360
something that we could do with the
technology that we developed that would

00:19:16.360 --> 00:19:19.950
create a commercially viable product
that turned out to be Flickr.

00:19:19.950 --> 00:19:23.373
&gt;&gt; Yeah, so ironically,
Game Neverending did end.

00:19:23.373 --> 00:19:24.105
&gt;&gt; Yep.

00:19:24.105 --> 00:19:29.620
&gt;&gt; So [LAUGH] how did you feel when you
realized you had to shut down the game?

00:19:31.480 --> 00:19:36.000
&gt;&gt; Horrible, but at the same time,
that kind of happened, but

00:19:36.000 --> 00:19:40.660
we were developing Flickr and the games
side-by-side for a couple of months.

00:19:40.660 --> 00:19:41.980
The decision to make Flickr and

00:19:41.980 --> 00:19:46.210
then its launch, three months,
separating those happened pretty quickly.

00:19:48.180 --> 00:19:50.240
But in that case, it felt like it was,

00:19:50.240 --> 00:19:53.260
there's a path forward,
the team got to stay together.

00:19:54.520 --> 00:19:58.510
We had disappointed a lot of
people who were playing it, but

00:19:58.510 --> 00:20:01.010
also a lot of them are like, but
cool, Flickr is interesting too and

00:20:01.010 --> 00:20:05.450
they just kind of migrated,
so that wasn't that bad.

00:20:05.450 --> 00:20:08.340
&gt;&gt; It's good, so you realize you
have to shut down a game, but

00:20:08.340 --> 00:20:12.010
not before flying to one last
video game conference in New York.

00:20:12.010 --> 00:20:14.420
What happens next?

00:20:14.420 --> 00:20:18.086
&gt;&gt; So it's a conference on law and
virtual worlds, and it's in New York,

00:20:18.086 --> 00:20:20.357
and flew from Vancouver
where I was living.

00:20:20.357 --> 00:20:25.066
And got food poisoning on the flight and I
don't want to get too vivid, but it's just

00:20:25.066 --> 00:20:30.100
like puking in the immigration hall at
JFK, puking in the cab on the freeway.

00:20:30.100 --> 00:20:34.931
Get to the hotel and step out of
the cab and puke all over the carpet,

00:20:34.931 --> 00:20:38.045
[LAUGH] so it's all right, a little vivid.

00:20:38.045 --> 00:20:41.437
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; I couldn't keep down anything,

00:20:41.437 --> 00:20:43.260
like ginger ale, water.

00:20:44.640 --> 00:20:50.360
And that night at like 3 in the morning
after being up with kind of feverish and

00:20:50.360 --> 00:20:55.310
frantic, wrote out the whole
first version of flicker,

00:20:55.310 --> 00:20:58.170
what it would be and how would take
advantage of those technologies.

00:20:58.170 --> 00:20:59.200
I will say this though

00:21:01.370 --> 00:21:04.330
that was the very first version which
was very different than one and

00:21:04.330 --> 00:21:09.280
ended up becoming It was not actually
very good That got us got us going.

00:21:09.280 --> 00:21:14.350
&gt;&gt; So I've am yet to have a battle
with food poisoning be so productive.

00:21:15.620 --> 00:21:17.760
So kudos to you there.

00:21:17.760 --> 00:21:22.210
You decided to focus on flicker
When you made that shift,

00:21:22.210 --> 00:21:27.200
did everyone on your team buy in
immediately or what was that process like?

00:21:27.200 --> 00:21:30.615
&gt;&gt; No so
I'm pretty democratic leader sometimes.

00:21:30.615 --> 00:21:34.600
[LAUGH] Maybe less so now than back then.

00:21:34.600 --> 00:21:40.294
But we had a vote and there was a tie and
I was like [SOUND] so I called.

00:21:40.294 --> 00:21:43.220
Erik Costello was actually one of
the founders of slack as well.

00:21:44.510 --> 00:21:48.380
And just like this and background lobbying
to get him to change his vote, so

00:21:48.380 --> 00:21:50.240
that we could go ahead with it.

00:21:52.470 --> 00:21:55.390
There's definitely like there's people
who are still interested in making

00:21:56.950 --> 00:22:00.010
the game and felt like it was
a shame to leave it behind.

00:22:00.010 --> 00:22:04.650
It was also I think, in terms of the
number of people online, way too early and

00:22:04.650 --> 00:22:07.630
the technology that was
available way too early.

00:22:07.630 --> 00:22:12.630
Like I think people forget 2002 was
the first year that any country

00:22:12.630 --> 00:22:16.550
that more than 50% internet penetration
at home and that was the Netherlands.

00:22:16.550 --> 00:22:19.370
So, and even then that was almost
all dialogue connectivity.

00:22:19.370 --> 00:22:22.130
So most Americans didn't
have Internet access.

00:22:22.130 --> 00:22:23.790
If they did, it wasn't working.

00:22:23.790 --> 00:22:25.680
It was very kind of narrowly prescribed.

00:22:25.680 --> 00:22:29.890
And if they did anything online,
it was like maybe check sports stores and

00:22:29.890 --> 00:22:31.276
stock prices or something like that.

00:22:31.276 --> 00:22:35.730
So there wasn't really a market for it.

00:22:35.730 --> 00:22:39.500
But, I just like making software
just in the same way that I did

00:22:39.500 --> 00:22:42.110
When I was seven years old and I think
everyone else on the team did too, so

00:22:42.110 --> 00:22:44.060
we just got to make
a different kind of software.

00:22:44.060 --> 00:22:49.700
And in the end it was, the game was,
play as a pretext for social interaction.

00:22:49.700 --> 00:22:51.650
Flickr was photography as a pretext for

00:22:51.650 --> 00:22:56.060
social interaction, is the first, actually
one of the, we got bought by Yahoo and

00:22:56.060 --> 00:22:58.970
one of the designers there called it,
massively multiplayer photo-sharing.

00:22:58.970 --> 00:23:01.510
Which I thought was pretty accurate
because it was our first Thing,

00:23:01.510 --> 00:23:05.170
other than websites that
I heard about later.

00:23:05.170 --> 00:23:09.760
Where you can put a photo online and
people can see it, and comment on it and

00:23:09.760 --> 00:23:13.820
have a title and description and
you can tag it and create groups and

00:23:13.820 --> 00:23:15.090
all that kind of stuff.

00:23:15.090 --> 00:23:18.840
So it was a social network that
revolved around photographs.

00:23:18.840 --> 00:23:23.810
I think we started right around
the same time as Facebook,

00:23:23.810 --> 00:23:28.210
when Facebook was still just in Harvard
for another six months or a year, and

00:23:28.210 --> 00:23:31.840
then just Ivys for
another close to a year after that.

00:23:31.840 --> 00:23:34.840
&gt;&gt; Cool, so not to spoil the story, but

00:23:34.840 --> 00:23:39.970
you eventually sell Flickr to Yahoo for
excess of $20 million.

00:23:39.970 --> 00:23:44.630
I'm curious, looking back now as you're
leading Slack what lessons from your time

00:23:44.630 --> 00:23:48.620
at Flickr have been most influential
on the leader that you are today?

00:23:50.100 --> 00:23:52.170
&gt;&gt; It's hard to say because it was so
long ago.

00:23:52.170 --> 00:23:56.480
We started development in 2003,
launched in 2004, and then

00:24:00.090 --> 00:24:05.823
The summer inside the winter break 2004
2005 there's this big Decision about

00:24:05.823 --> 00:24:07.989
whether we're going to take VC funding or
we're going to get bought by Yahoo.

00:24:07.989 --> 00:24:11.613
So it was 2005 to 2008 and
I was there, and

00:24:11.613 --> 00:24:16.362
there's definitely not something
that stands out like the thing

00:24:16.362 --> 00:24:21.279
that I learned there other than how
hard it is to get something done or

00:24:21.279 --> 00:24:25.244
how hard it is,
I mean me that's that is a good lesson,

00:24:25.244 --> 00:24:30.400
how hard it is to get something done
in an organization of that size.

00:24:30.400 --> 00:24:33.864
Yeah, it was about 12,000 people.

00:24:33.864 --> 00:24:36.837
And I think there's a couple things that
were wrong with it at that time, but

00:24:36.837 --> 00:24:38.987
the biggest one was it had
basically stopped growing.

00:24:38.987 --> 00:24:44.457
And in an environment where the pie isn't
growing anymore, suddenly the kind of

00:24:44.457 --> 00:24:50.120
game theoretic, the calculus, especially
among executives is very zero sum.

00:24:50.120 --> 00:24:53.530
So it was like people battling
each other internally.

00:24:53.530 --> 00:24:57.670
We even forget that for a second,
any company of any organization of 10,000

00:24:57.670 --> 00:25:02.850
people and it requires such
an extraordinary injection of a will

00:25:02.850 --> 00:25:07.950
to make anything happen that most things
are for all practical purposes impossible.

00:25:09.660 --> 00:25:15.630
&gt;&gt; Okay, so we fast forward to 2012,
you have left Yahoo, which by the way,

00:25:15.630 --> 00:25:21.070
if you have not read Seward's resignation
letter from Yahoo, please Google it.

00:25:21.070 --> 00:25:27.300
He likens himself to attend Smith or
named Brad, it's quite hilarious.

00:25:27.300 --> 00:25:32.300
Since 2012, you're starting another video
game company this time, Tiny Speck.

00:25:32.300 --> 00:25:35.120
At Tiny Speck,
you launched a game called Glitch,

00:25:35.120 --> 00:25:38.540
what gave you more confidence in
the gaming area this time around?

00:25:38.540 --> 00:25:43.220
&gt;&gt; So it's 2009 that we started the
company, and 2012 is when we shut it down.

00:25:43.220 --> 00:25:45.830
But it's the same group of people,
[INAUDIBLE] the four of us who had worked

00:25:45.830 --> 00:25:48.900
at Flicker, and
we all worked at Yahoo together.

00:25:48.900 --> 00:25:53.283
But 2002 to 2009 was pretty amazing
time in the history of the Internet,

00:25:53.283 --> 00:25:55.741
and suddenly everyone had Internet access.

00:25:55.741 --> 00:26:02.210
And there were phones that were capable
of Internet access, Blackberries and

00:26:02.210 --> 00:26:08.104
Trios, there were a lot of people had
high speed Internet by that time.

00:26:08.104 --> 00:26:12.998
The world of open source software
specifically to support development

00:26:12.998 --> 00:26:15.607
on the Internet we had just exploded,

00:26:15.607 --> 00:26:19.210
so there was really not
much available in 2002.

00:26:19.210 --> 00:26:23.205
But by 2009, we had a very robust mature,

00:26:23.205 --> 00:26:27.305
Apache foundation, and all of these
great networking technologies,

00:26:27.305 --> 00:26:30.455
people's computers are much faster,
and there's many more people online.

00:26:30.455 --> 00:26:33.497
We were much more experienced,
it was very easy to raise money.

00:26:33.497 --> 00:26:37.827
If you just like look at any factor in
a giant matrix of like things that would

00:26:37.827 --> 00:26:40.520
lead this to be a good plan or
to be successful.

00:26:40.520 --> 00:26:45.501
We had shifted from a two out of ten,
to an eight, nine or ten out of ten,

00:26:45.501 --> 00:26:49.920
except that that idea was still
not very commercially viable.

00:26:49.920 --> 00:26:52.962
[LAUGH] Same idea, like better graphics.

00:26:52.962 --> 00:26:56.390
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; Do you think that idea is ever going to

00:26:56.390 --> 00:26:58.093
be commercially viable?

00:26:58.093 --> 00:27:02.830
&gt;&gt; Well, I mean, so we could have kept
going and paid all of our salaries and

00:27:02.830 --> 00:27:07.101
been happy, and it would have been
interesting but we had taken,

00:27:07.101 --> 00:27:09.989
by that point, $17 million in VC money.

00:27:09.989 --> 00:27:15.161
So I felt like it would have been
an aberration of the responsibility,

00:27:15.161 --> 00:27:19.282
that kind of contract we made
with them to just do that, so

00:27:19.282 --> 00:27:22.780
we had to think of something else.

00:27:22.780 --> 00:27:24.540
&gt;&gt; So you said 17 and a half million,

00:27:24.540 --> 00:27:29.680
and funding you had around 45
employees around the time?

00:27:29.680 --> 00:27:32.750
So you find yourself in
a familiar situation and

00:27:32.750 --> 00:27:34.700
you have to shut down that game again,

00:27:34.700 --> 00:27:37.970
what was the toughest part about that
decision the second time around?

00:27:37.970 --> 00:27:41.660
&gt;&gt; So the second time around, it was very
different because it wasn't just, hey,

00:27:43.270 --> 00:27:48.530
everyone, we are now as a group and
a switch working on because there was

00:27:48.530 --> 00:27:53.520
animators, and musicians, and writers,
and illustrators, level designers.

00:27:53.520 --> 00:27:57.379
A whole bunch of people who just didn't
have skills that were transferable to so

00:27:57.379 --> 00:28:02.970
a lot of people were going to get
laid off, I think there was 35.

00:28:02.970 --> 00:28:08.840
And in the three and a half years that
we had been running the various versions

00:28:08.840 --> 00:28:13.570
of Glitch, there wasn't like pretty
strong and very active, robust community.

00:28:13.570 --> 00:28:17.020
There wasn't like a couple hundred
people who tested Game Neverending,

00:28:17.020 --> 00:28:21.330
you could just start using Flickr,
they were going to disappear.

00:28:21.330 --> 00:28:24.060
And I think this is a hard
thing to relate to or

00:28:24.060 --> 00:28:27.010
understand if you haven't gone through it,
and it's

00:28:28.830 --> 00:28:33.120
maybe something that hasn't happened in
a long time and might not happen again.

00:28:33.120 --> 00:28:38.100
Like Tumblr seems like the last platform
that had close communities like that,

00:28:38.100 --> 00:28:41.790
whereas now, everything, like Instagram
is just one world connected, so

00:28:41.790 --> 00:28:43.740
Facebook is another world,
Twitter is like everyone.

00:28:43.740 --> 00:28:46.130
And there are definitely, sub communities.

00:28:46.130 --> 00:28:50.150
But when communities exist in
a one specific platform, and

00:28:50.150 --> 00:28:53.720
that platform disappears, it is a little
bit like that moment in the first

00:28:53.720 --> 00:28:55.600
Star Wars when Alderaan gets blown up.

00:28:55.600 --> 00:29:00.030
Like it is just that society,
that little culture, those

00:29:00.030 --> 00:29:04.300
relationships just will not exist anymore,
so it is really like that was very sad.

00:29:04.300 --> 00:29:09.680
But obviously for me, first of all, it is
embarrassing, I did all this press and

00:29:09.680 --> 00:29:12.730
made all these claims, I had to
convince all these people to come and

00:29:12.730 --> 00:29:14.820
anytime you got new press it was
I had to convince them I'd do it.

00:29:14.820 --> 00:29:18.450
Every time I got investment,
I had to convince them to do it, but

00:29:18.450 --> 00:29:21.000
more than anything else I had to convince
all these people to come work on this.

00:29:21.000 --> 00:29:24.240
And I followed that story many times

00:29:24.240 --> 00:29:28.760
but the day that I made
the announcement internally

00:29:28.760 --> 00:29:31.530
called this all hands, some people already
a little bit apprehensive because we've

00:29:31.530 --> 00:29:35.420
been through a couple of different like,
here's the last thing we're going to try.

00:29:35.420 --> 00:29:38.520
There's wasn't a day when
we normally had no hands.

00:29:38.520 --> 00:29:44.647
And I locked eyes with someone as soon
as they started talking, who two or

00:29:44.647 --> 00:29:50.673
three months before I had convinced to
move to a new city with his wife and

00:29:50.673 --> 00:29:56.016
two-year-old daughter away
from where his in-laws lived.

00:29:56.016 --> 00:29:59.432
Their in-laws were hoping to take care
of the kid, he moved to the city,

00:29:59.432 --> 00:30:02.961
bought a house, and I was going to tell
him that he didn't have a job anymore.

00:30:02.961 --> 00:30:07.601
Those are really, really, really hard,
I mean I think that that's a,

00:30:07.601 --> 00:30:12.546
like the impact on me reputationally or
financially was, in the grand scheme

00:30:12.546 --> 00:30:17.510
of things, relatively insignificant,
I haven't just bounce back again.

00:30:18.950 --> 00:30:23.610
But that's more than just disappointing
someone like I was going to come

00:30:23.610 --> 00:30:27.510
meet you for dinner and I bailed at
the last minute or something like that,

00:30:27.510 --> 00:30:30.200
this is like I convince you to
alter the course of your life

00:30:31.590 --> 00:30:35.660
in a really significant way and
then didn't It didn't happen.

00:30:35.660 --> 00:30:40.190
So that was very, very difficult, in
the end knew like a little bit of positive

00:30:40.190 --> 00:30:44.460
news because we had five and
a half million dollars left of that money.

00:30:44.460 --> 00:30:46.640
We're able to shut down in
a relatively elegant way,

00:30:46.640 --> 00:30:51.580
so we made a portfolio site
which had everyone's resumes and

00:30:51.580 --> 00:30:55.030
did a bunch of reference letter writing
and kind of career coaching and.

00:30:57.210 --> 00:30:58.744
Helps get everyone a job,

00:30:58.744 --> 00:31:02.808
in most cases a better job than they
had when they were working for us.

00:31:02.808 --> 00:31:07.488
And we were able to give customers
the choice of their money back where we

00:31:07.488 --> 00:31:11.778
could donate it to charity or
whatever, but and that one person,

00:31:11.778 --> 00:31:15.530
Tim Leffler,
ended up joining slop bucket a year later.

00:31:15.530 --> 00:31:18.780
So that part all worked out too, but
doesn't mitigate at all like what it felt

00:31:18.780 --> 00:31:23.070
like in that moment,
it was really pretty terrible.

00:31:23.070 --> 00:31:28.170
&gt;&gt; That's pretty heavy, so
how do you go from that terrible moment to

00:31:28.170 --> 00:31:32.920
launching Slack which reached
a billion dollar valuation in

00:31:32.920 --> 00:31:38.700
record setting pace of eight months?

00:31:38.700 --> 00:31:40.187
&gt;&gt; That worked out super well [INAUDIBLE].

00:31:40.187 --> 00:31:43.856
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; We had developed a system that was

00:31:43.856 --> 00:31:45.014
the proto-Slack.

00:31:45.014 --> 00:31:47.750
Again, 92,
I mentioned that one of the software

00:31:49.100 --> 00:31:52.930
network tools that I used was
called IRC or Internet Relay Chat.

00:31:52.930 --> 00:32:00.110
And we used IRC at the company that made
Glitch, and it's a very old technology.

00:32:00.110 --> 00:32:05.792
So it's With most messaging systems,
and probably every messaging

00:32:05.792 --> 00:32:09.161
system you've ever used, there's a concept
of what's called store and forward.

00:32:09.161 --> 00:32:12.319
And if I want to send a message to you but
I can't reach you right now,

00:32:12.319 --> 00:32:15.709
there's no connection to your endpoint,
your client or your device,

00:32:15.709 --> 00:32:19.350
it will just be held and then forwarded
to you the next time you connect.

00:32:19.350 --> 00:32:20.290
But IRC didn't have that.

00:32:20.290 --> 00:32:22.320
If you weren't connected at
the moment that I sent the message,

00:32:22.320 --> 00:32:23.700
you would just never receive it.

00:32:23.700 --> 00:32:25.890
So we built the system
to log the messages.

00:32:25.890 --> 00:32:27.200
But once we had
the messages in a database,

00:32:27.200 --> 00:32:28.360
we wanted to be able to search them.

00:32:28.360 --> 00:32:30.140
So we built search on top of that.

00:32:30.140 --> 00:32:32.390
And then bit by bit,
kind of feature by feature,

00:32:32.390 --> 00:32:34.540
we built things to integrate
with our file server.

00:32:34.540 --> 00:32:38.855
So when someone uploaded a file,
it would get announced in the IRC or

00:32:38.855 --> 00:32:42.840
if an alert went off in our data center,
then that would get put into IRC.

00:32:42.840 --> 00:32:48.780
And slowly we developed the system which
was really the foundation of all of

00:32:48.780 --> 00:32:52.480
the ways in which the company communicated
and was really beneficial until we

00:32:52.480 --> 00:32:56.936
realized none of us are ever going to work
without something like this ever again.

00:32:56.936 --> 00:33:00.890
Other teams of [INAUDIBLE] software
developers would probably like it as well,

00:33:00.890 --> 00:33:03.190
and so
we decided that's what we're going to do.

00:33:03.190 --> 00:33:06.100
And we thought that one day
in the fullness of time,

00:33:06.100 --> 00:33:10.680
if we had every single person
who could possibly use this,

00:33:10.680 --> 00:33:15.240
we would have a $100 million in revenue
and thereby be a billion dollar company.

00:33:15.240 --> 00:33:17.673
And that just happened very quickly.

00:33:17.673 --> 00:33:19.039
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; Yeah,

00:33:19.039 --> 00:33:20.817
I definitely think the math checks out.

00:33:20.817 --> 00:33:25.670
So at Slack, you created a product that
not a lot of companies knew they needed.

00:33:25.670 --> 00:33:27.210
How did you convince them otherwise?

00:33:27.210 --> 00:33:31.901
&gt;&gt; That was tough, the first first three
or four external teams to use Slack.

00:33:31.901 --> 00:33:38.437
It took dozens of tries like going
to their office and showing them.

00:33:38.437 --> 00:33:41.126
And I think we learned a lot there about,

00:33:44.182 --> 00:33:47.632
Marketing probably isn't the right term.

00:33:47.632 --> 00:33:51.559
And I think we actually had this problem
a little bit with Flickr because Facebook

00:33:51.559 --> 00:33:55.488
came out and just stole the social photo
sharing market while Flickr was trying to

00:33:55.488 --> 00:33:58.838
decide whether it wanted to be social
photo sharing or a community for

00:33:58.838 --> 00:34:01.460
people who are interested in photography.

00:34:01.460 --> 00:34:04.650
But if you can't explain what you're doing
well enough that someone to whom you

00:34:04.650 --> 00:34:07.920
explain it can go on to explain it to
someone else, then it's a real problem.

00:34:07.920 --> 00:34:10.140
because otherwise, you're going to
have to do all the explaining.

00:34:11.540 --> 00:34:16.260
So we struggled to figure out the way to
talk about it, what advantages it had,

00:34:16.260 --> 00:34:17.670
what it was for.

00:34:17.670 --> 00:34:20.750
But when it's net new and
it's not replacing something else,

00:34:20.750 --> 00:34:21.921
it's very difficult.

00:34:21.921 --> 00:34:27.247
I don't know if it's still frequently
read but there's a classic book

00:34:27.247 --> 00:34:32.240
in marketing called Positioning,
Jack Trout and Al Ries I think.

00:34:32.240 --> 00:34:36.400
And one of things they talked about is if
something is a new concept for you, it's

00:34:36.400 --> 00:34:41.190
almost impossible for you to get purchased
in somebody's brain, in somebody's mind.

00:34:41.190 --> 00:34:45.900
You have to find something else
that already exists there and

00:34:45.900 --> 00:34:49.990
then alter that idea,
which is why you hear Uber for whatever.

00:34:49.990 --> 00:34:52.370
Because if you had to explain
the whole thing from scratch,

00:34:52.370 --> 00:34:53.770
it's just very difficult.

00:34:53.770 --> 00:34:57.219
It's why you hear movie pitches
that are Jaws meets Star Wars or

00:34:57.219 --> 00:34:58.465
something like that.

00:34:58.465 --> 00:35:00.110
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; And

00:35:00.110 --> 00:35:03.340
it's much easier to get that
than to start from from scratch.

00:35:03.340 --> 00:35:06.740
But it was like it was a real slog
to get anyone to even try it.

00:35:06.740 --> 00:35:09.860
And the encouraging thing was
once people started trying it,

00:35:09.860 --> 00:35:11.846
they almost invariably stuck with it.

00:35:11.846 --> 00:35:14.211
They've logged in every day,
became like it was for

00:35:14.211 --> 00:35:16.770
us the foundation of
how they communicated.

00:35:16.770 --> 00:35:21.560
&gt;&gt; And so from 2014 to 2017,
there was limited competitors for

00:35:21.560 --> 00:35:25.210
Slack given that it was
such an innovative idea.

00:35:25.210 --> 00:35:29.660
But in 2017, when Slack had
more than 100 million revenue,

00:35:29.660 --> 00:35:32.490
which you predicted 650 employees and

00:35:32.490 --> 00:35:37.660
evaluation of around $5 billion,
Microsoft launched their team's app.

00:35:37.660 --> 00:35:40.378
How did you feel when you found
out that app was coming out?

00:35:44.161 --> 00:35:49.238
&gt;&gt; I think mostly just good
because it validated the idea and

00:35:49.238 --> 00:35:53.797
we had some advanced notice
that it was coming out.

00:35:53.797 --> 00:35:58.061
because we've worked with Microsoft
on something, like early stuff with

00:35:58.061 --> 00:36:03.190
Microsoft research on building
question answering box for first lock.

00:36:03.190 --> 00:36:08.330
And had a pretty good relationship with a
guy named Qi Lu who was a software exec at

00:36:08.330 --> 00:36:10.530
Yahoo, who was the CTO of Microsoft.

00:36:12.030 --> 00:36:15.041
Left around them to go
be to take over Baidu.

00:36:15.041 --> 00:36:19.514
But we weren't especially worried
just because when it was,

00:36:19.514 --> 00:36:23.900
before it was first announced,
it was called Skype teams and

00:36:23.900 --> 00:36:29.232
had a pretty different approach and
was just so far behind us from a product

00:36:29.232 --> 00:36:33.899
perspective that we weren't
worried about people switching.

00:36:33.899 --> 00:36:38.658
&gt;&gt; Okay, so now in 2020, how do you
feel about Microsoft's Team app?

00:36:38.658 --> 00:36:41.558
&gt;&gt; Well, it got a lot better
as Microsoft [INAUDIBLE].

00:36:41.558 --> 00:36:44.278
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; I think there is a bunch of things

00:36:44.278 --> 00:36:51.910
that, That make it much more of
a challenge for us today than it was then.

00:36:51.910 --> 00:36:56.750
And it's not just that it's better
because it's actually not better enough

00:36:56.750 --> 00:37:01.030
compared to how it was that any of our
large customers could switch to it.

00:37:01.030 --> 00:37:06.745
Our biggest single user is IBM,

00:37:06.745 --> 00:37:11.745
somewhere close to 300,000 daily active
users over 10,000 workspaces and Teams is

00:37:11.745 --> 00:37:15.765
limited to 5,000 users per workspace and
you can't federate them together.

00:37:15.765 --> 00:37:20.242
So there'd just be no way to support
that Structures and what didn't work for

00:37:20.242 --> 00:37:23.222
them and
there's many other things that are very,

00:37:23.222 --> 00:37:26.552
very fundamental limitations
over 5,000 people.

00:37:26.552 --> 00:37:30.434
You can have 200 channels and
if you want to add the 200 first,

00:37:30.434 --> 00:37:33.551
you have to add,
delete one with all of the messages,

00:37:33.551 --> 00:37:37.428
but none of that really matters in
the face of if you want to be able to

00:37:37.428 --> 00:37:41.990
collaborate on the word doc with
track changes send it back and forth.

00:37:41.990 --> 00:37:44.276
You're lawyers working on a contract or

00:37:44.276 --> 00:37:46.967
marketing people working
on a press release and

00:37:46.967 --> 00:37:51.220
you are an Office 365 customer,
you more or less have to use Teams now.

00:37:51.220 --> 00:37:55.940
And the 100 million users of Skype for
Business are being migrated

00:37:55.940 --> 00:38:00.410
over to the Teams because Skype for
Business is being shut down.

00:38:00.410 --> 00:38:06.211
So there's a bunch of things
that are kind of force it but

00:38:06.211 --> 00:38:11.392
maybe most fundamental
is we have 12 million.

00:38:11.392 --> 00:38:14.280
I don't even remember what our public
number is on daily active users.

00:38:14.280 --> 00:38:15.958
12?
Yeah, okay, so-

00:38:15.958 --> 00:38:17.444
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]

00:38:17.444 --> 00:38:18.252
&gt;&gt; 12 million and

00:38:18.252 --> 00:38:20.905
there's at least 200 million people for
whom FAQ or

00:38:20.905 --> 00:38:23.213
something like it is
the preferred way to work so

00:38:23.213 --> 00:38:26.468
200 million people whose working
lives are mediated by email.

00:38:26.468 --> 00:38:29.935
And they are moving over
I think is inevitable.

00:38:29.935 --> 00:38:36.904
So that's 6% which means 94%
of people don't use it yet.

00:38:36.904 --> 00:38:41.290
And if you don't use it and
you don't have any idea and and

00:38:41.290 --> 00:38:44.990
you hear that there's two alternatives,
one's Slack and one's Teams.

00:38:44.990 --> 00:38:48.910
And because you're an Office 65 customer,
Teams is already free and

00:38:48.910 --> 00:38:51.790
integrated with all of
your Microsoft tools.

00:38:51.790 --> 00:38:54.120
Then why would you even evaluate Slack?

00:38:54.120 --> 00:38:59.409
Or Microsoft was aggressive in a way
that I think was surprising to a lot

00:38:59.409 --> 00:39:05.597
of people even who watched that company
closely, like putting out a press release

00:39:05.597 --> 00:39:11.448
with our daily active users in it during
our quiet period, post the listing.

00:39:11.448 --> 00:39:14.834
But if they can put out a press
release in tank or share price, and

00:39:14.834 --> 00:39:19.104
you're not watching this stuff very
closely, you don't have a fighting degree

00:39:19.104 --> 00:39:24.170
of resolution, then you might think as
a customer, why would I invest in Slack?

00:39:24.170 --> 00:39:25.772
They're just going to be out
of business in three years.

00:39:25.772 --> 00:39:27.410
Microsoft's going to inevitably kill them.

00:39:28.660 --> 00:39:31.662
That doesn't, it would be a waste
of my time to even look at it.

00:39:31.662 --> 00:39:35.680
And it's not like at that point, the fact
that they are different really matters to

00:39:35.680 --> 00:39:38.670
you because you don't use either and
it's not really replacing anything.

00:39:38.670 --> 00:39:40.330
So it's much more of a threat now.

00:39:40.330 --> 00:39:43.376
I think we underestimated
the degree of importance.

00:39:43.376 --> 00:39:46.971
The Financial Times Person of
the Year was Satya Nadella.

00:39:46.971 --> 00:39:48.935
And so there's a big write-up of that.

00:39:48.935 --> 00:39:54.339
And there's six consecutive paragraphs
that are about Slack and there's no other,

00:39:54.339 --> 00:40:00.180
maybe the names of some competitor
companies are mentioned in one sentence.

00:40:00.180 --> 00:40:05.162
Here and there, but
like it's the biggest chunk of it.

00:40:05.162 --> 00:40:10.425
And so, I think that's because of
all the things that can be used

00:40:10.425 --> 00:40:16.579
as leverage by Microsoft to expand
relationships inside our businesses.

00:40:16.579 --> 00:40:21.865
Exchange, the email server, and
the fact that people are very

00:40:21.865 --> 00:40:27.463
used to Outlook is like the principle
one that kind of gets them in.

00:40:27.463 --> 00:40:30.572
And that makes it difficult for
people to switch.

00:40:30.572 --> 00:40:32.924
And if people stop paying
attention to email,

00:40:32.924 --> 00:40:36.845
if email declines in relative importance
compared to other software we use,

00:40:36.845 --> 00:40:39.279
that's a really difficult position for
them.

00:40:39.279 --> 00:40:41.950
So from their perspective, I think, and

00:40:41.950 --> 00:40:45.319
this is what she knew we
thought back like 2016.

00:40:45.319 --> 00:40:46.502
This is in fact,

00:40:46.502 --> 00:40:52.170
successful to the maximum extent that's
an existential threat to Microsoft.

00:40:52.170 --> 00:40:54.922
But I don't think that's
actually true because so

00:40:54.922 --> 00:40:58.314
many other things would change in
the world on the path to that.

00:40:58.314 --> 00:41:01.230
But I think that is a thought process.

00:41:01.230 --> 00:41:05.793
&gt;&gt; So, despite selling out and
actually becoming a consultant,

00:41:05.793 --> 00:41:09.414
I studied mechanical
engineering in undergrad.

00:41:09.414 --> 00:41:13.516
And as a black engineer, I really
appreciate what Slack has been doing from

00:41:13.516 --> 00:41:16.066
the perspective of diversity and
inclusion.

00:41:16.066 --> 00:41:20.184
Back in 2015, Slack shared a diversity
report which revealed that most of their

00:41:20.184 --> 00:41:24.186
black employees were in technical roles,
contrasted with a lot of companies that

00:41:24.186 --> 00:41:27.566
will hire black individuals
primarily into administrative roles.

00:41:27.566 --> 00:41:31.946
And in that same report,
45% of managers were females.

00:41:31.946 --> 00:41:36.442
So to a room full of individuals who
will start companies or be at companies,

00:41:36.442 --> 00:41:39.765
what advice you have for
creating diverse workplaces?

00:41:39.765 --> 00:41:42.455
&gt;&gt; Start early,
I think that's the biggest thing.

00:41:42.455 --> 00:41:47.513
When we were 20 employees, I would say,

00:41:47.513 --> 00:41:51.034
between 20 and 30 maybe.

00:41:51.034 --> 00:41:53.718
Wow, there's a lot of white dudes.

00:41:53.718 --> 00:41:56.939
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; And in that case,

00:41:56.939 --> 00:42:01.437
it wasn't too late, but
it was close to too late.

00:42:01.437 --> 00:42:04.200
It was like more of a slog to get started.

00:42:04.200 --> 00:42:08.593
Because what happened was,
we have one black woman engineer, and

00:42:08.593 --> 00:42:12.759
then another one comes to interview and
she sees the first one.

00:42:12.759 --> 00:42:16.362
And suddenly it's like a completely
different assessment of what's going on

00:42:16.362 --> 00:42:17.791
here, and then there's two.

00:42:17.791 --> 00:42:19.839
And then the third one comes for
interview,

00:42:19.839 --> 00:42:23.282
if feels like there's community, and
people talk, and have a network.

00:42:23.282 --> 00:42:27.875
So, getting started early I think
is the most important thing.

00:42:27.875 --> 00:42:35.260
I think it can be a kind of
a fraught topic I think for people.

00:42:35.260 --> 00:42:37.375
People aren't sure what to say or
they're uncomfortable.

00:42:37.375 --> 00:42:41.129
And I think there is
a really pervasive and

00:42:41.129 --> 00:42:46.063
incorrect belief that you
would have to lower the bar to

00:42:46.063 --> 00:42:50.677
hire someone who isn't
the canonical candidate,

00:42:50.677 --> 00:42:54.444
the archetypal candidate for this role.

00:42:54.444 --> 00:42:57.305
I think that's usually not the truth for
two reasons.

00:42:57.305 --> 00:43:00.249
One is, you just have to look harder,
you're going to see more people.

00:43:00.249 --> 00:43:02.684
And in fact, that can raise the bar.

00:43:02.684 --> 00:43:07.452
But people have different
challenges in their life, and

00:43:07.452 --> 00:43:13.117
I don't think you can perfectly
understand someone's background

00:43:13.117 --> 00:43:17.801
just from their gender identity,
or their ethnicity.

00:43:17.801 --> 00:43:23.004
But on the whole, for
a woman to get to a certain place in

00:43:23.004 --> 00:43:27.998
her career,
they had to work a lot harder than a man.

00:43:27.998 --> 00:43:32.806
For a black engineer to get to
a certain place in his or her career,

00:43:32.806 --> 00:43:37.624
they had to work a lot harder than,
this is where it gets fraught.

00:43:37.624 --> 00:43:42.437
In Ben Horowitz's words, Jewish, Chinese,
or Indian guys in Silicon Valley because

00:43:42.437 --> 00:43:45.893
there's just like these networks
that are very powerful.

00:43:45.893 --> 00:43:48.844
And you have an enormous advantage.

00:43:48.844 --> 00:43:51.906
So for
two people with equivalent credentials,

00:43:51.906 --> 00:43:56.387
the person who's probably going to
be more talented, more capable, and

00:43:56.387 --> 00:44:01.090
had to overcome more obstacles to get to
where they are is going to be the one who

00:44:01.090 --> 00:44:04.960
doesn't have the traditional
archetypal presentation.

00:44:04.960 --> 00:44:06.963
&gt;&gt; It's amazing.
Thank you for sharing that, I'm sure,

00:44:06.963 --> 00:44:08.278
I hope people were taking notes.

00:44:08.278 --> 00:44:10.894
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; So

00:44:10.894 --> 00:44:13.386
we've gone through
the journey of your career.

00:44:13.386 --> 00:44:14.751
You started as a web developer.

00:44:14.751 --> 00:44:17.434
You founded a company that
was acquired by Yahoo.

00:44:17.434 --> 00:44:21.675
You launched a second company that raised
more than $1.2 billion in venture funding,

00:44:21.675 --> 00:44:23.863
and you eventually took
that company public.

00:44:23.863 --> 00:44:26.060
You've been a leader
throughout this entire time.

00:44:26.060 --> 00:44:29.228
What has changed about your leadership
style and what stayed the same?

00:44:34.282 --> 00:44:41.960
&gt;&gt; [INAUDIBLE] It's always hard
to really assess yourself.

00:44:41.960 --> 00:44:45.689
I think I'm relatively self aware even
of those things that don't work and

00:44:45.689 --> 00:44:48.363
it doesn't matter that I
know that they don't work.

00:44:48.363 --> 00:44:49.352
I still can't change them.

00:44:49.352 --> 00:44:55.212
But there is a difference in the mechanics

00:44:55.212 --> 00:45:00.745
of being a leader at different scales.

00:45:00.745 --> 00:45:03.091
Because when it's 20 people and
we all kind of know each other,

00:45:03.091 --> 00:45:03.968
it's very different.

00:45:03.968 --> 00:45:08.355
I say something and
then we argue about it, and

00:45:08.355 --> 00:45:12.529
maybe I change my mind, and it's all good.

00:45:12.529 --> 00:45:16.546
There's a power dynamic where
I'm the CEO and whoever, and

00:45:16.546 --> 00:45:18.531
everyone else is not the CEO.

00:45:18.531 --> 00:45:23.228
But when you get to 500 people or
1,000 people, where we're at now,

00:45:23.228 --> 00:45:26.491
a couple of thousand people,
it's very different.

00:45:26.491 --> 00:45:28.288
Someone's going to come
into your presentation for

00:45:28.288 --> 00:45:29.449
some new product development.

00:45:29.449 --> 00:45:33.388
And it's like a relatively new designer or
engineer, or

00:45:33.388 --> 00:45:36.434
product manager you've never met before.

00:45:36.434 --> 00:45:41.080
To me, this is just like a one more
30-minute meeting in my schedule,

00:45:41.080 --> 00:45:42.153
which is [SOUND].

00:45:42.153 --> 00:45:46.656
And for them, this is something
they've been thinking about for weeks.

00:45:46.656 --> 00:45:50.338
They probably talked to their spouse
about it, and they're either excited or

00:45:50.338 --> 00:45:52.136
they're nervous, they want to know.

00:45:52.136 --> 00:45:57.470
So the degree of impact
that my words have is like,

00:45:57.470 --> 00:46:00.518
from the perspective of me,

00:46:00.518 --> 00:46:05.734
crazy out of proportion
to the amount of action.

00:46:05.734 --> 00:46:09.684
So in other words, it's like a super,

00:46:09.684 --> 00:46:14.368
super powerful microphone
on at all the time.

00:46:14.368 --> 00:46:19.714
So if anything's negative or critical,
if I have a relationship with someone

00:46:19.714 --> 00:46:24.657
that I've worked together for
a long time, it's not really a big deal.

00:46:24.657 --> 00:46:28.666
If it's someone that, that might be the
only time they interact with me ever in

00:46:28.666 --> 00:46:30.206
their whole career at Slack.

00:46:30.206 --> 00:46:33.510
Or it might be the first interaction
they've ever had with me, and

00:46:33.510 --> 00:46:36.053
they might not have another one for
several years.

00:46:36.053 --> 00:46:37.603
It carries a huge amount of weight.

00:46:37.603 --> 00:46:42.058
So it's hard because,
is that the amount of time we have like,

00:46:42.058 --> 00:46:44.760
including questions from everyone?

00:46:44.760 --> 00:46:45.617
&gt;&gt; That is.

00:46:45.617 --> 00:46:46.377
&gt;&gt; Okay.

00:46:46.377 --> 00:46:48.688
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; I'll try and

00:46:48.688 --> 00:46:50.442
be a little bit more concise.

00:46:50.442 --> 00:46:55.874
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; I

00:46:55.874 --> 00:46:59.768
remember reading on Twitter people
arguing about Warren's tax plan.

00:46:59.768 --> 00:47:04.620
And someone said something like,
if Dwayne The Rock Johnson just paid this

00:47:04.620 --> 00:47:08.485
tax at this rate,
then we'd have an extra $50 million.

00:47:08.485 --> 00:47:13.711
And then that's how much it would
cost to solve the water crisis.

00:47:13.711 --> 00:47:16.663
And it struck me as just
a totally absurd argument because

00:47:16.663 --> 00:47:19.503
federal budget is something
like $2.7 trillion.

00:47:19.503 --> 00:47:24.172
So 50 million bucks plus or minus is not
the reason why that doesn't get solved.

00:47:24.172 --> 00:47:26.125
It doesn't get solved for
all kinds of reasons.

00:47:26.125 --> 00:47:31.065
And people think that way all the time,
that it's just like money or

00:47:31.065 --> 00:47:32.805
it's just resources.

00:47:32.805 --> 00:47:34.505
For anything significant to happen,

00:47:34.505 --> 00:47:38.445
certainly anything that involves dozens of
people, let alone hundreds or thousands.

00:47:38.445 --> 00:47:42.098
The amount of will that has to go in,
the amount of like selling,

00:47:42.098 --> 00:47:46.023
the amount of vision, the amount
of like coercion and cajoling, and

00:47:46.023 --> 00:47:48.610
the amount of encouragement, and support.

00:47:48.610 --> 00:47:50.886
All of those things is just enormous.

00:47:50.886 --> 00:47:57.776
So, the game does change as the company
gets bigger and things evolve.

00:47:57.776 --> 00:48:02.335
And I think probably only
gets more difficult, but.

00:48:02.335 --> 00:48:04.890
That's what I've been
learning this whole time

00:48:06.420 --> 00:48:10.030
is how to do that without
like crushing people.

00:48:10.030 --> 00:48:13.120
How to do that without creating

00:48:13.120 --> 00:48:18.020
an environment where it's entirely
top down like iron fist from above.

00:48:19.400 --> 00:48:21.970
And it can be tough
because I have a different

00:48:21.970 --> 00:48:24.230
perspective than anyone else
because I see everything.

00:48:24.230 --> 00:48:28.241
So I talked to someone in engineering,
I get a head of sales reports to me,

00:48:28.241 --> 00:48:31.736
head of marketing reports to me,
head of finance reports to me,

00:48:31.736 --> 00:48:33.702
our general counsel reports to me.

00:48:33.702 --> 00:48:38.820
So I have a very different perspective
than what is ultimately relatively narrow.

00:48:38.820 --> 00:48:40.636
And generally,
a better idea of what we need to do.

00:48:40.636 --> 00:48:43.665
because I also talk to customers more
than pretty much anyone else other than

00:48:43.665 --> 00:48:44.540
the sales person.

00:48:44.540 --> 00:48:46.796
And I talk to our investors
more than anyone else.

00:48:46.796 --> 00:48:49.946
And I talk to our board
more than anyone else.

00:48:49.946 --> 00:48:52.870
Yeah, it's really, but

00:48:52.870 --> 00:48:57.810
I think there are better sources
than me for like top ten tips.

00:48:59.030 --> 00:49:03.692
Because none of them
seem that simple to me.

00:49:03.692 --> 00:49:08.455
And it's not that they're not out there,
but the real challenge of leadership and

00:49:08.455 --> 00:49:13.530
maybe there's one book I would recommend,
which is Leadership and Self-Deception.

00:49:13.530 --> 00:49:17.133
The real fundamental challenge of
leadership is the same as the fundamental

00:49:17.133 --> 00:49:19.000
challenge of just being a human being.

00:49:19.000 --> 00:49:27.092
And I think that's, This will
sound a little bit weird, perhaps.

00:49:27.092 --> 00:49:32.007
But living with an open heart and
not seeing other people as, on one hand,

00:49:32.007 --> 00:49:35.733
either instruments that can
be used to your advantage or

00:49:35.733 --> 00:49:40.356
obstacles that are in the way of
something that you're trying to do.

00:49:40.356 --> 00:49:43.596
Which is the default judgement of all

00:49:43.596 --> 00:49:48.101
people with whom you are not
close instinctually.

00:49:48.101 --> 00:49:52.061
So like you're on a Southwest flight and
people are still boarding and

00:49:52.061 --> 00:49:54.488
the middle seat between you isn't taken.

00:49:54.488 --> 00:49:57.804
And you're like, please don't take this
seat, please don't take this seat,

00:49:57.804 --> 00:49:59.090
please don't take this seat.

00:49:59.090 --> 00:50:03.950
A person has a whole life and
their own ambitions and

00:50:03.950 --> 00:50:05.760
desires and heartaches and
stuff like that.

00:50:05.760 --> 00:50:08.160
But to you, in that moment, they're just

00:50:08.160 --> 00:50:12.560
a potential pain in the ass that might
take the middle seat next to you.

00:50:12.560 --> 00:50:16.030
And that's pervasive, and when you're
really trying to accomplish something,

00:50:16.030 --> 00:50:22.090
it can be very tempting to see people
as either instruments or obstacles.

00:50:22.090 --> 00:50:26.540
&gt;&gt; Amazing, so we're going to go to the
audience for, we probably have time for

00:50:26.540 --> 00:50:27.359
about two questions.

00:50:28.540 --> 00:50:29.096
So?

00:50:35.924 --> 00:50:36.560
&gt;&gt; Hi, Stuart.

00:50:36.560 --> 00:50:38.903
Really appreciate your
comments on diversity, so

00:50:38.903 --> 00:50:41.631
it makes me feel better about
my Stanford Slack addiction.

00:50:41.631 --> 00:50:43.251
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; My question is,

00:50:43.251 --> 00:50:46.584
what were some of the key and
best things you did on the product and

00:50:46.584 --> 00:50:48.820
design front in the early days of Slack?

00:50:48.820 --> 00:50:53.595
So for example, how much of Slack's
success do you attribute to your personal

00:50:53.595 --> 00:50:58.820
eye for design, versus hiring the best
designers, versus feature prioritization,

00:50:58.820 --> 00:51:02.540
or even just the insight
about having a personality?

00:51:02.540 --> 00:51:05.790
Curious what advice you'd share there for
aspiring entrepreneurs.

00:51:05.790 --> 00:51:11.180
&gt;&gt; Yeah, so I'm not sure I want it like
slice it up by who gets more credit.

00:51:11.180 --> 00:51:12.860
But I think the fundamental approach was

00:51:13.860 --> 00:51:16.510
how much easier can we
make people's lives.

00:51:16.510 --> 00:51:20.699
And when I look at other products,
it's really,

00:51:20.699 --> 00:51:25.608
I'm trying to think of the shortest
version of this I can.

00:51:25.608 --> 00:51:28.685
It can be an amazing app, and if
the password reset thing doesn't work, and

00:51:28.685 --> 00:51:31.380
I need to reset my password to use it,
then I'm just locked out.

00:51:31.380 --> 00:51:36.990
So there's very basic, fundamental
things that you have to get right,

00:51:36.990 --> 00:51:39.570
and which aren't the interesting ones.

00:51:39.570 --> 00:51:42.030
In fact, someone Tweeted something
the other day which I liked that kind of

00:51:42.030 --> 00:51:44.160
illustrates this from
a different perspective.

00:51:44.160 --> 00:51:47.300
And it's someone asking Ray Kroc,
the kind of founder and

00:51:47.300 --> 00:51:49.980
the first CEO of McDonald's,
why was McDonald's so successful?

00:51:49.980 --> 00:51:54.883
And he says, because we have clean
bathrooms, and they say, that's easy.

00:51:54.883 --> 00:51:55.980
That's so simple.

00:51:55.980 --> 00:51:57.490
That doesn't explain it.

00:51:57.490 --> 00:51:59.420
And Ray Kroc said,
are your bathrooms clean?

00:52:00.900 --> 00:52:04.830
And it actually is like a challenge.

00:52:04.830 --> 00:52:06.440
So there's some real fundamentals.

00:52:06.440 --> 00:52:10.910
But the things that we did that were most
successful were those things which made

00:52:10.910 --> 00:52:12.870
life more convenient for people.

00:52:12.870 --> 00:52:16.580
And one of those was for example, typing
your password on your phone is a pain,

00:52:16.580 --> 00:52:18.606
so we'll send you a magic
link that logs you in.

00:52:18.606 --> 00:52:24.850
Or because for complex reasons, most
people wanted to have notifications for

00:52:24.850 --> 00:52:26.840
every message in Slack
when they first signed up.

00:52:26.840 --> 00:52:29.100
So they felt comfortable and
knew how it worked.

00:52:29.100 --> 00:52:31.540
But we didn't think
that was a good way for

00:52:31.540 --> 00:52:34.136
them to set their preferences long-term.

00:52:34.136 --> 00:52:37.364
After a few notifications,
we would interject and say,

00:52:37.364 --> 00:52:40.334
would you like to switch
to our preferred settings?

00:52:40.334 --> 00:52:44.175
And that kind of thoughtfulness or
consideration,

00:52:44.175 --> 00:52:47.491
that kind of thinking of
yourself as a host and

00:52:47.491 --> 00:52:53.008
the customers as your guests,
is the secret, as it were, to good design.

00:52:53.008 --> 00:52:57.589
&gt;&gt; And we have time for one more question.

00:52:57.589 --> 00:52:58.902
&gt;&gt; Can you hear me?

00:52:58.902 --> 00:52:59.435
&gt;&gt; Yeah.

00:52:59.435 --> 00:53:01.750
&gt;&gt; Thank you so much for
taking the time to speak with us today.

00:53:01.750 --> 00:53:05.610
So in your answers, it was really clear
that like, what you've been interested in

00:53:05.610 --> 00:53:08.260
is kind of facilitating communication
through different mediums.

00:53:08.260 --> 00:53:11.673
Whether it be video games,
photography, now Slack.

00:53:11.673 --> 00:53:14.518
Was that understanding clear
to you through your journey?

00:53:14.518 --> 00:53:17.913
And if not, was there like an aha
moment that you realized this

00:53:17.913 --> 00:53:20.732
was what the issue you really
wanted to work on was?

00:53:20.732 --> 00:53:23.849
&gt;&gt; Yeah, well, so
the desire was there, but

00:53:23.849 --> 00:53:27.670
I didn't think I recognized
it as one single thing.

00:53:27.670 --> 00:53:30.624
because Slack is also a massively
multiplayer workplace software.

00:53:30.624 --> 00:53:35.057
I mean, that's kind of the principal
distinction between Slack and

00:53:35.057 --> 00:53:37.440
pretty much every other tool.

00:53:37.440 --> 00:53:39.960
And it is very much like
the games that we wanted to play.

00:53:39.960 --> 00:53:42.894
You take objects and
you can manipulate them and

00:53:42.894 --> 00:53:45.912
distribute them and
form groups and all of that.

00:53:45.912 --> 00:53:49.926
But I don't really recognize them as being
fundamentally similar until much later.

00:53:49.926 --> 00:53:54.549
The thing that I thought as being the
common thread was just it's all software.

00:53:54.549 --> 00:53:59.268
And software that groups of people use
together to me is the most interesting

00:53:59.268 --> 00:54:03.175
type of challenges,
because it has all the regular challenges

00:54:03.175 --> 00:54:07.547
of scalability on the one hand and
design and usability and the other.

00:54:07.547 --> 00:54:11.393
But social dynamics because of
the feedback loop where the output of

00:54:11.393 --> 00:54:14.293
the system can also be an input
to the system are much

00:54:14.293 --> 00:54:18.159
more difficult to design for and
therefore much more interesting.

00:54:20.020 --> 00:54:22.360
&gt;&gt; Amazing, thank you all for
the questions.

00:54:22.360 --> 00:54:23.310
Dharma-
&gt;&gt; Yeah.

00:54:23.310 --> 00:54:24.396
&gt;&gt; It's been a pleasure chatting with you.

00:54:24.396 --> 00:54:27.116
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; It's not often that we get time with

00:54:27.116 --> 00:54:28.894
a classically trained philospher.

00:54:28.894 --> 00:54:32.490
So I have a new spin on our
typical lightning round.

00:54:32.490 --> 00:54:35.297
I'm going to ask you a few
questions that keep me up at night.

00:54:35.297 --> 00:54:36.224
Is that okay?
&gt;&gt; Yeah.

00:54:36.224 --> 00:54:36.971
&gt;&gt; Cool.

00:54:36.971 --> 00:54:40.263
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; So we'll start with an easy one,

00:54:40.263 --> 00:54:41.326
is water wet?

00:54:41.326 --> 00:54:42.135
&gt;&gt; Yes.

00:54:42.135 --> 00:54:45.772
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; Okay, if soap hits the floor,

00:54:45.772 --> 00:54:49.415
is the floor clean or is the soap dirty?

00:54:49.415 --> 00:54:50.623
&gt;&gt; Soap's dirty.

00:54:50.623 --> 00:54:51.439
&gt;&gt; Yeah.

00:54:51.439 --> 00:54:54.840
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; Dirty soap, all right.

00:54:54.840 --> 00:54:57.880
I have to make sure I pronounce
this one the right way.

00:54:57.880 --> 00:54:58.600
&gt;&gt; All right.

00:54:58.600 --> 00:55:03.030
&gt;&gt; Does expecting the unexpected
make the unexpected expected?

00:55:05.330 --> 00:55:05.830
&gt;&gt; No.

00:55:07.130 --> 00:55:08.434
&gt;&gt; No, we're going with no?

00:55:08.434 --> 00:55:12.370
&gt;&gt; Yeah.
&gt;&gt; Okay, final one, you're ready?

00:55:12.370 --> 00:55:14.631
&gt;&gt; Yeah.
&gt;&gt; One word or less.

00:55:14.631 --> 00:55:18.061
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; What is the meaning of life?

00:55:18.061 --> 00:55:19.894
&gt;&gt; I thought the question was one word or
less.

00:55:19.894 --> 00:55:28.307
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; For

00:55:28.307 --> 00:55:31.224
those of you who read Douglas Hofstader,

00:55:31.224 --> 00:55:35.820
if you're familiar with the history
of Buddhism, I'll say mu.

00:55:35.820 --> 00:55:36.560
&gt;&gt; Mu?

00:55:36.560 --> 00:55:37.821
&gt;&gt; Yeah.

00:55:37.821 --> 00:55:40.333
&gt;&gt; We have a minute and 20 seconds left.

00:55:40.333 --> 00:55:42.581
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; So I would love to hear more.

00:55:42.581 --> 00:55:43.796
&gt;&gt; It's funny.

00:55:43.796 --> 00:55:47.088
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH] When I said before,

00:55:47.088 --> 00:55:53.051
contemporary Anglo-American philosophy
is really boring on the one hand.

00:55:53.051 --> 00:55:55.872
And you take away all the subject matter,

00:55:55.872 --> 00:56:01.199
there's an enormous corpus of research and
argument and even books written.

00:56:01.199 --> 00:56:05.834
But certainly like thousands
of papers on the question of,

00:56:05.834 --> 00:56:08.624
do holes exist, like H-O-L-E.

00:56:08.624 --> 00:56:11.439
Like is there such a thing as a hole,
or is that the absence of something?

00:56:11.439 --> 00:56:13.502
And I swear that is a giant argument.

00:56:13.502 --> 00:56:17.298
So, I find it easier just to come
down on the side of one or the other.

00:56:17.298 --> 00:56:22.363
The meaning of life, I don't know,
to love one another.

00:56:22.363 --> 00:56:23.767
&gt;&gt; Okay, I like that one.

00:56:23.767 --> 00:56:25.739
Ladies and gentlemen, Stuart.

00:56:25.739 --> 00:56:31.222
&gt;&gt; [APPLAUSE]
&gt;&gt; Thank you.

00:56:31.222 --> 00:56:36.268
[MUSIC]

